WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:03.530
- Over the last two and a half years

00:00:03.530 --> 00:00:07.030
89 songs have made their
way into the top five slots

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:10.070
of the Billboard Hot 100 charts.

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.910
For this episode, the team at Sound Field

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.830
cracked open every one of these songs

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.220
to identify certain trends

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:16.840
that are common in music right now.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.110
What are chart-topping artist doing today

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.781
versus in the past.

00:00:20.781 --> 00:00:23.198
(mic static)

00:00:24.822 --> 00:00:25.910
(music notes flip)

00:00:25.910 --> 00:00:27.830
These days if you want to top the charts,

00:00:27.830 --> 00:00:30.950
it's better to use the
sad, mysterious tones

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.840
of the minor keys in modes

00:00:32.840 --> 00:00:35.050
rather than the more regular major keys

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:38.370
that have historically been
used at the top of the charts.

00:00:38.370 --> 00:00:40.904
In this graph, we can see that
of all the tonalities used,

00:00:40.904 --> 00:00:43.280
harmonic minor and natural minor,

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:46.190
as well as the minor modes
of dorian and phrygian,

00:00:46.190 --> 00:00:48.340
outed the appearance of major keys

00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:50.990
in the lydian mode by almost double.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:52.720
This is uncommon for pretty much

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.790
any other era of western popular music.

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:58.550
Even more surprising, is
the rise of phrygian mode

00:00:58.550 --> 00:01:01.973
in modern pop hits, 7%
of the songs used it.

00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:04.490
And while that's not a huge number itself,

00:01:04.490 --> 00:01:06.440
it becomes gigantic when you consider

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:08.460
that the overall usage of phrygian

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:13.460
across all popular music
ever, is well south of 1%.

00:01:13.599 --> 00:01:15.300
(music notes flip)

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:18.370
- In lyric writing, or any
kind of writing or poetry,

00:01:18.370 --> 00:01:20.150
a trochee is when a stressed syllable

00:01:20.150 --> 00:01:22.180
is followed by an unstressed syllable.

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:25.090
So like, trochee, Mona Lisa,

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.000
or bad and boujee.

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:28.480
These trochees are in contrast

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.840
to the more common
cousins, iambic phrases,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.947
like, "I'm bad at love,

00:01:32.947 --> 00:01:35.230
"She walks in beauty like the night",

00:01:35.230 --> 00:01:37.890
or "Havana ooh na na".

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:39.990
What we're hearing all over
the pop charts these days

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:43.660
is that trochees are often
being sped up, double time.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.670
So what was two eighth notes before

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:48.240
becomes two sixteenth
notes followed by a rest.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.290
We call these rapid trochees

00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:51.489
and they sound like this.

00:01:51.489 --> 00:01:53.154
♪ stuntin', popping bottles ♪

00:01:53.154 --> 00:01:56.266
♪ Uh oh, uh oh, uh oh, uh oh ♪

00:01:56.266 --> 00:01:57.671
♪ Ain't no stoppin' ♪

00:01:57.671 --> 00:01:58.945
♪ Album droppin' ♪

00:01:58.945 --> 00:02:01.018
♪ Do my back like I do these records ♪

00:02:01.018 --> 00:02:02.970
♪ Walkin' past the mirror, ooh ♪

00:02:02.970 --> 00:02:05.040
♪ I'm the hottest in the street ♪

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:07.880
♪ Banging body, spicy mami, hot tamale ♪

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:10.970
Actually, maybe we should
just call these Cardi trochees

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:13.400
because the last four examples
were of Cardi B using them.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:15.780
Shout to Adam Neely's
video on Scotch Snaps

00:02:15.780 --> 00:02:17.020
where he digs into this more.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:19.709
- Teenage mutant ninja turtles.

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:20.542
(music notes flip)

00:02:20.542 --> 00:02:22.200
- So what does the Billboard Hot 100

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.270
have against guitars these days.

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:25.450
Well, let's first mention

00:02:25.450 --> 00:02:27.300
that two of the most common keys

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.711
that guitars have
traditionally written songs in

00:02:29.711 --> 00:02:32.076
are E and A major.

00:02:32.076 --> 00:02:34.547
♪ We're gonna rock around
the clock tonight ♪

00:02:34.547 --> 00:02:37.307
♪ Aye Oh, let's go ♪

00:02:37.307 --> 00:02:40.004
♪ Layla ♪

00:02:40.004 --> 00:02:42.180
♪ Yes, I'm back in black ♪

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.021
- Now, let's look at what's
going on in today's charts.

00:02:45.021 --> 00:02:47.630
Here we can see that while A and E

00:02:47.630 --> 00:02:49.800
have healthy showings for minor keys,

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.700
not one song to top the charts

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:53.788
was written in A or E major.

00:02:53.788 --> 00:02:56.450
(music notes flip)

00:02:56.450 --> 00:02:58.050
- Now let's take a look at the tempos

00:02:58.050 --> 00:03:00.270
that chart toppers have been using lately.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:04.320
Okay, so we have a small
spike from 67 to 70 VPM

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.430
but the real story here, is this big gap

00:03:06.430 --> 00:03:08.810
from 109 to 118 VPM,

00:03:08.810 --> 00:03:10.920
with just Lil Baby and
Gunna's Drip Too Hard

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:14.320
sitting here at 112 like
they didn't get the memo.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:16.720
But seriously, this is not typical.

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:17.830
The most common tempo

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.890
in the Rolling Stone greatest
500 songs of all time,

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:22.750
was 112 VPM.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.770
I guess modern pop
listeners just don't respect

00:03:24.770 --> 00:03:26.350
these tempos anymore.

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:27.812
Maybe they went out of style.

00:03:27.812 --> 00:03:29.220
(music note flips)

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:31.830
- One thing that still hasn't
gone out of style in pop music

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:36.450
is the pre-dominate use
of the major key triads.

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:39.650
Major one, four, five, and minor six.

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.600
These chords still dominate the harmony

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:42.880
in a lot of songs.

00:03:42.880 --> 00:03:46.046
In fact, 28% of the songs in major keys

00:03:46.046 --> 00:03:47.616
only use these chords.

00:03:47.616 --> 00:03:48.449
(music notes flip)

00:03:48.449 --> 00:03:51.050
- In a very general sense,
pop songs have evolved

00:03:51.050 --> 00:03:52.868
from having one repeated section,

00:03:52.868 --> 00:03:56.920
to two sections, to having three sections,

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:00.890
often organized as verses,
choruses, and a bridge.

00:04:00.890 --> 00:04:02.080
As songs kept evolving,

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.470
we started to see more
interlude sections into the mix,

00:04:04.470 --> 00:04:07.140
such as repeated intros,
instrumental solos,

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.220
and pre-choruses.

00:04:08.220 --> 00:04:11.420
And finally, the post-chorus
is now becoming pretty common.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.730
But where a pre-chorus warms up the stage,

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:16.690
a post-chorus cools things
off before the song returns,

00:04:16.690 --> 00:04:19.150
usually to a verse,
but sometimes a bridge,

00:04:19.150 --> 00:04:20.320
or another section.

00:04:20.320 --> 00:04:22.049
Songs with post-chorus's include:

00:04:22.049 --> 00:04:24.747
♪ We ain't ever getting older ♪

00:04:24.747 --> 00:04:27.897
♪ Girlfriend, girlfriend, girlfriend ♪

00:04:27.897 --> 00:04:28.978
♪ Quiero ver bailar tu pelo ♪

00:04:28.978 --> 00:04:31.180
♪ Quiero ser tu ritmo ♪

00:04:31.180 --> 00:04:32.090
(music notes flip)

00:04:32.090 --> 00:04:34.450
- I'm sorry to report
that the modern pop song

00:04:34.450 --> 00:04:38.070
still has a long way to go
on the gender parody front.

00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.450
Let's take a look.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.510
So not only were there twice
as many male solo tracks

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:44.770
as there were solo female tracks

00:04:44.770 --> 00:04:48.613
but only 8% of tracks had
no male vocalist, at all.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:50.453
(music notes flip)

00:04:50.453 --> 00:04:52.580
Traditionally, both
western classical music

00:04:52.580 --> 00:04:56.840
and pop have really liked
this home and away interplay.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:58.830
Almost always beginning and ending

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.340
on clear-cut home chords.

00:05:01.340 --> 00:05:03.910
But it's really becoming
less and less important

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:05.170
as the years go by.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.910
And this shows up all
over on today's charts.

00:05:07.910 --> 00:05:10.530
Take the loop from Drake's
hit, Nice For What,

00:05:10.530 --> 00:05:11.720
it's based off of a loop

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.668
he got from Lauryn
Hills's song, Ex-Factor.

00:05:14.668 --> 00:05:16.434
♪ Care for me, care for me ♪

00:05:16.434 --> 00:05:17.267
♪ I know you care for me ♪

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:19.240
Here are the chords in the sample.

00:05:19.240 --> 00:05:20.750
E flat major,

00:05:20.750 --> 00:05:21.920
E flat major seven,

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:23.240
D flat major,

00:05:23.240 --> 00:05:24.861
and F minor.

00:05:24.861 --> 00:05:25.800
So, the thing about this is

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.110
that there is a lack of
reinforcement in the bass,

00:05:29.110 --> 00:05:31.950
there aren't any cadences really,

00:05:31.950 --> 00:05:34.350
and also, there aren't dominate chords.

00:05:34.350 --> 00:05:35.250
These are all things

00:05:35.250 --> 00:05:37.950
that would indicate a key center for us.

00:05:37.950 --> 00:05:40.503
For example, if we're in
the key of A flat major,

00:05:41.660 --> 00:05:42.617
these chords,

00:05:42.617 --> 00:05:45.290
(piano music)

00:05:45.290 --> 00:05:46.867
might lead,

00:05:46.867 --> 00:05:47.700
(piano music)

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:48.700
back to the tonic.

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:49.861
Back to home-base.

00:05:49.861 --> 00:05:50.840
We don't have those signs,

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:51.709
we just have.

00:05:51.709 --> 00:05:54.209
(piano music)

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.196
Are we in

00:05:58.196 --> 00:05:59.430
(piano music)

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:00.411
F?

00:06:00.411 --> 00:06:01.710
(piano music)

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:03.320
Natural minor or aeolian?

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:05.604
Are we in D flat?

00:06:05.604 --> 00:06:07.500
(piano music)

00:06:07.500 --> 00:06:08.391
D flat lydian?

00:06:08.391 --> 00:06:10.891
(piano music)

00:06:13.990 --> 00:06:15.210
So it's really hard to say.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:16.580
Towards the middle of the song

00:06:16.580 --> 00:06:17.415
we do hear..

00:06:17.415 --> 00:06:19.915
(piano music)

00:06:21.423 --> 00:06:22.577
In the base, a little bit.

00:06:22.577 --> 00:06:23.881
♪ And you showin' off, ♪

00:06:23.881 --> 00:06:24.976
♪ but it's alright ♪

00:06:24.976 --> 00:06:26.243
♪ It's a short life ♪

00:06:26.243 --> 00:06:30.700
But still, it's not providing
a strong key center for us.

00:06:30.700 --> 00:06:31.884
- Dean, can you introduce yourself,

00:06:31.884 --> 00:06:34.420
let some people know who
you are and what you do.

00:06:34.420 --> 00:06:35.790
- Yeah, my name's Dean Olivet.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:38.570
I just statistify music, you know.

00:06:38.570 --> 00:06:40.350
I'm a trend spotter, I suppose.

00:06:40.350 --> 00:06:42.390
- As far as total ambiguity,

00:06:42.390 --> 00:06:43.790
I feel like the tone ambiguity

00:06:43.790 --> 00:06:45.120
it be happening on accident

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:45.953
a lot of times.

00:06:45.953 --> 00:06:47.007
- Yeah.

00:06:47.007 --> 00:06:48.859
- Or it's like, it's not intentional,

00:06:48.859 --> 00:06:51.010
a compositional decision
that's being made.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:54.070
- Well yeah, just trying to
find what sounds good to you

00:06:54.070 --> 00:06:57.114
rather than what you've
been trained to do.

00:06:57.114 --> 00:06:59.158
- And you think about
Lil Jon and Eastide Boyz,

00:06:59.158 --> 00:07:01.972
Da, da, da, da, dae, da, da, da, da, dae,

00:07:01.972 --> 00:07:03.750
da, da, and they just
do it an octave higher.

00:07:03.750 --> 00:07:04.760
Da, da, da, da.

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:07.130
But, it's like, that became a massive hit.

00:07:07.130 --> 00:07:10.920
- What makes sense to
me is to use the analagy

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.860
of people trying on different clothes.

00:07:13.860 --> 00:07:18.230
People are more experimental
with everything from color,

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:19.740
to the shape of things,

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:21.840
and I think that's what
happens with music too.

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:23.370
- It's like back in the day you had like,

00:07:23.370 --> 00:07:24.450
functional clothes, like, okay,

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.720
I'm going to school I'm gonna
where my school clothes.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:27.650
Those have a function.

00:07:27.650 --> 00:07:28.590
I'm gonna wear my work clothes,

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:31.130
I'm sleeping, I'm gonna
wear my pajamas, you know.

00:07:31.130 --> 00:07:32.640
Clothes ambiguity is like,

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:34.720
what if I just wear my
pajamas to work or, you know?

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:37.420
And that's parallel with music.

00:07:37.420 --> 00:07:40.387
Functionality isn't as
important as it used to be.

00:07:40.387 --> 00:07:41.320
(music notes flip)

00:07:41.320 --> 00:07:43.980
- Tight lyrics and ad libs
are nothing new to pop music.

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:45.770
But never before have
they been so prevalent

00:07:45.770 --> 00:07:47.091
across the top of the charts.

00:07:47.091 --> 00:07:48.156
